// Polygon-fill pixel screen, 64x64, 64 lanes.  GENERATED by
// gen.py -- edit the generator, not this file.
//
// One color output per pixel; the save wires each to one brick's property
// changer.  Outputs carry NO side annotation: one would place a physical
// rerouter pin per port (4096 pins in an 8k-unit line, wrecking the prefab
// bounds), and nobody outside needs them -- the wall taps the inner output
// gates directly by remote wire.
//
// Protocol: pushShape latches a shape -- its point colours,
// fill-vs-stroke, slot, transform and parent -- and drawAllShapes
// paints the CURRENT shape: the latched one, else the last
// one again.  The point count is the array's length (the
// connector delivers a tight list; a direct driver sends
// exactly as many points as the shape has).  An ODD length
// means the points are (x,y,z) triples instead of (x,y) pairs:
// the chip then transforms each point's own z and PROJECTs the
// result (screen = EYE * world / (EYE + world.z), the eye at
// z = -EYE), so a cube of world points draws as a cube.  A 2D
// array is always even, so one bit of length separates the two
// -- with no port to add and no triangulation to fake: the CPU
// sends vertices and per-point colours and the chip does the
// rest.  A 3D shape therefore carries an ODD number of points,
// which for a quad means the closed outline the stroke
// protocol already uses (repeat the first point: a degenerate
// closing edge that fills nothing and changes no bounds).  At
// most 10 points fit (30 floats in the 32-slot staging array).
// shapeColors is
// optional and per-point: one colour per point, white where
// absent.  shapePosition, shapeRotation, shapeScale and parentShapeId are
// optional too: the staged points are transformed once per
// staged shape -- shapeScale, rotate about the origin by
// shapeRotation.z (radians), translate -- after composing the
// staged transform through every ancestor, so a child follows
// its parent (and grandparent) with one push to the parent's
// slot; parent 0 (or a dead/missing parent) is the identity,
// and unwired vectors read zeroes (shapePosition/shapeRotation zero are
// the identity, shapeScale zeroes mean identity too).  brightness
// is a live user slider the connector never drives (1..100,
// capped; unwired reads 0.0, the 100 default): it scales every
// painted colour immediately: its edge reruns the retained
// recomposite without the black clear and refreshes the last
// immediate shape, so the wall follows with no push and no
// drawAllShapes.
// true scanline interpolation -- each row's span takes the
// colours of its two crossing edges (lerped along the edges by
// row), then lerps across the span -- so a polygon with
// per-point colours fills with a smooth gradient.  Strokes
// interpolate along the segment (the quad's corner colours)
// and across its width the same way.  A fill closes its
// outline implicitly; a stroke runs consecutive pairs with no
// wrap, so the driver closes a wireframe by repeating the first
// point and leaves an open curve open (butt caps; joins overlap
// in the same colour, so need no mitres).  A single point
// strokes as a 2x2 dot.  Bounds are clipped; a fully off-screen
// shape paints nothing.  Brick colors persist until rewritten,
// so there is no keepalive and no expiry: the chip emits one
// burst per drawAllShapes and is silent otherwise.
//
// The fill is a scanline walk.  At the arm the points are read from the
// array into per-edge scalars (base x/y, x-per-y slope, y range);
// each row's span is the min and max of the crossings of the edges that
// straddle it, which is the exact fill of a convex polygon -- a concave
// outline's rows fill their hull, so the driver decomposes one into
// convex pieces.  One span is painted per tick per row pipeline --
// two rows per tick, so a burst costs one tick per two rows,
// not per 64 px.
//
// The stroke is a second walk over the same lanes.  At the arm the points
// land in a preserved store (replays restore from it, never from a dirty
// array) and in a shift register the walk consumes pair by pair; each
// pair's 2px quad -- offset by the unit normal, extended a pixel along the
// tangent so joints overlap -- fills through the same row-span machinery
// at 4 edges deep.  A burst costs one tick per two rows; segment
// transitions and padded points ride the same advances.
//
// Tuning (top of gen.py): LANES is the speed knob.  Placement is
// physical: the wall builder lays the 64x64 bricks, so there are no
// screen coordinates here.

@layout("cube")

const SW = 64
const SH = 64
const NP = 4096
const SWF = 64.0
const MAXP = 16
const MAXP3 = 10

@left in shapePoints: float[]
@left in shapeColors: color[]
@left in pushShape: bool
@left in shapeWireMode: bool
@left in shapeId: float
@left in drawAllShapes: bool
@left in clearAllShapes: bool
@left in shapePosition: vector
@left in shapeRotation: vector
@left in shapeScale: vector
@left in parentShapeId: float
@left in addToShapeInTicks: float
@left in brightness: float

